# Enhanced C Style Guide

This guide builds upon the existing C style guidelines while incorporating advanced practices for code quality and maintainability.

## Core Style Rules

### Indentation and Spacing
- Use spaces for indentation
- Set tab width to 4 spaces
- Maximum line length: 120 characters
- Use consistent vertical spacing for readability

### Naming Conventions
- Use snake_case for functions, variables, and file names
- Use UPPER_CASE for constants and macros
- Use meaningful, descriptive names
- Prefix functions with module name where appropriate

### Function Design
- Keep functions small and focused on a single task
- Limit function length to 50 lines where possible
- Use descriptive names indicating action performed
- Document parameters and return values

## Memory Management

### Allocation and Deallocation
- Always pair memory allocations with corresponding deallocations
- Check allocation success before using pointers
- Use consistent error handling for allocation failures
- Document ownership transfer where applicable

### Pointer Safety
- Initialize pointers to NULL
- Check pointers before dereferencing
- Clear pointers after freeing
- Document pointer ownership expectations

## Documentation Requirements

### Function Documentation

```c
/**
 * Brief description of function purpose
 * 
 * Detailed description if needed
 * 
 * @param param1 Description of first parameter
 * @param param2 Description of second parameter
 * @return Description of return value
 * 
 * @see AGE-DOC-ID123
 */
```

### File Header Documentation

```c
/**
 * @file filename.c
 * @brief Brief description of file purpose
 * @details Detailed description if needed
 * 
 * @author Original Author
 * @date YYYY-MM-DD
 * 
 * @see AGE-DOC-ID123
 */
```

## Testing Requirements

### Test Coverage
- Every function must have corresponding test cases
- Tests must cover standard, edge, and failure cases
- Memory allocation failures must be tested
- Boundary conditions must be verified

### Test Implementation
- Place tests in corresponding test directory
- Name test files to match implementation files
- Organize tests by functionality
- Document test purpose and expectations

## Code Modification Guidelines

- Do not modify code that already works unless explicitly required
- Avoid duplicating existing functionality; reuse working components
- Write comprehensive tests for all new or modified functionality
- Commit frequently to maintain a reliable project history

## Implementation Notes

This enhanced style guide extends the existing C style guidelines. When conflicts occur, this guide takes precedence due to its higher priority level. 